  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe persistent mid/upper ridge may finally shift eastward slightly \nas we move through the next 24 hours. We expect another deeply mixed \npbl with little inhibition and we could see isolated storms develop \nalong a subtle convergence zone within the sfc trough axis \nstretching across parts of central and south central KS during the \nlate afternoon and evening hours on Thursday. Any high-based storm \nmay bring a threat of damaging winds and perhaps some dry lighting, \nbut confidence in storm coverage remains low. Other elevated storms \nmay develop during the evening hours as the LLJ ramps up across \nparts of central and south central Kansas. Confidence in max \ntemperatures remains fairly low given the uncertainty in how \nfar south the diffuse boundary makes it.\n\nAs we move into Friday-Saturday, we continue to see uncertainty \nwith the frontal boundary position which may be forced further south \ndepending on the amount of deep moist convection. Isolated storms \nalong the sfc trough axis will continue to be possible each day and \nlingering cloud debris could have an impact on high temperatures \nthrough the period. Low pops were maintained with a continuation of \ntriple digit heat through the period.\n\nSunday-Wednesday...A shortwave trough is progged to sweep across the \nNorthern Plains late in the weekend helping to drive a cold front \nsouth across the Central Plains. This front may become a focus for \nshowers and thunderstorms on Sunday-Sunday night with mostly dry and \nseasonable temperatures returning across the region on Monday.\n\nThe mid/upper ridge is progged to amplify across the Southern Plains \nas we move towards the middle of the week bringing rising \nheights/increasing thickness and rising temperatures back to the \narea. Some low pops were maintained but confidence in wetting \nprecipitation remains rather low at this time.",
